Hi Series Eight team,

We sent you a client security audit for oatly.com with a published grade of D=
 (50/100). That grade was wrong. Our scoring model at the time penalised ever=
y finding flatly and counted best-practice configuration items as vulnerabili=
ties. Re-scoring the same scan under the corrected model gives B (85/100).

Three finding classes drove the wrong grade: the missing HSTS header (HTTP St=
rict Transport Security), the missing HTTP response hardening headers (CSP, X=
-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, =
COOP) and the missing mail security records (BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT).

That scan returned no critical or high finding, so the published failure did =
not describe exploit risk.

The rule now: best-practice items are reported but never scored, a scan with =
no critical or high finding cannot score below 60, and repeated findings of t=
he same class are charged at a reduced rate under a per-class cap.

Next step: reply to this note and we will re-issue the oatly.com audit with t=
he corrected grade and score. No change to the client's configuration is requ=
ired to correct the score.

Best regards,
Carson Haynes
